WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.508
<v Instructor>So this lecture I love because,</v>

2
00:00:01.508 --> 00:00:04.380
it really shows you what kind of decisions you have

3
00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:06.350
to make as a solution architect.

4
00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:09.470
What kind of implication it has on the very simple thing.

5
00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:11.330
Such as how to update an application

6
00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:12.840
in a Auto Scaling group.

7
00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:14.300
You may think this is simple.

8
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:15.900
So we have our clients connected

9
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:18.560
to Application Load Balancer.

10
00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:20.130
That Application Load Balancer redirects traffic

11
00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:21.510
to our Auto Scaling group.

12
00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:23.826
Which currently is running all our EC2 instances

13
00:00:23.826 --> 00:00:26.290
on these same launch templates.

14
00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:29.540
We would like to do is to update our Auto Scaling group

15
00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:30.650
and for now what we'll actually do

16
00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:32.560
is to have a new launch configuration.

17
00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:34.690
This is the way we want to update it, okay.

18
00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:36.450
So let's see all the options we have

19
00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:39.280
and what they mean in terms of architecture

20
00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:40.830
and implications for the way

21
00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:42.890
you're rolling out your application.

22
00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:45.240
As a game, if you want to stop the video right now,

23
00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:48.300
just pause it and then draw something on a sheet of paper.

24
00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:49.810
I asked you to find how many ways

25
00:00:49.810 --> 00:00:51.820
you can update this Auto Scaling group.

26
00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:52.653
What kind of strategy you have

27
00:00:52.653 --> 00:00:54.240
and then what kind of architectures

28
00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.010
are coming through it with it.

29
00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:57.260
If you don't, that's okay.

30
00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:58.600
Let's get started.

31
00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:00.380
So the first thing is that

32
00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:02.770
we may want to keep that Auto Scaling group.

33
00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:04.390
So we keep the Auto Scaling group

34
00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:07.410
but we're going to create a new launch template.

35
00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:08.480
What this will do is that,

36
00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:11.800
it will go ahead and create new EC2 instances

37
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:14.090
that are launched using this new launch templates

38
00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:15.980
and we may need to double the capacity

39
00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:19.170
of our Auto Scaling group for these instances to be created.

40
00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:20.810
So in the meantime because they belong

41
00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:22.280
to the same Auto Scaling group,

42
00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:24.320
they will belong to the same target group.

43
00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:27.220
So the Application Load Balancer, will redirect request

44
00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:29.410
to all these two instances at the same time.

45
00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:33.620
So we have both versions of our application sending data

46
00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:36.310
and responding to our ALB request.

47
00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:37.860
This is fine and then when we're done,

48
00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:40.080
we can terminate the EC2 instances

49
00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:43.260
of our launch template number one if we are confident enough

50
00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:45.940
that our version number two is working great.

51
00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:47.780
So this is one way of doing things

52
00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:49.720
and I think it works great, okay.

53
00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:50.703
But here you have to realize

54
00:01:50.703 --> 00:01:53.930
that you have the same target group.

55
00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:57.340
Traffic is going to be spread across all your versions.

56
00:01:57.340 --> 00:01:58.710
That's number one.

57
00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:01.450
Number two is, okay we have the same architecture.

58
00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:02.520
We have our first Auto Scaling group

59
00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:06.200
but now we decided to create a new Auto Scaling group.

60
00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:08.120
Alongside creating this new Auto Scaling group,

61
00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:11.000
we are creating a second target group.

62
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.360
So now we have our EC2 instances

63
00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:15.590
with a launch template on v2.

64
00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:18.720
We are able to do at the ALB level,

65
00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:22.290
a traffic split between the different two target groups.

66
00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:24.290
So it's possible for us in this case

67
00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:26.086
to just send a small amount of traffic

68
00:02:26.086 --> 00:02:27.850
to the Auto Scaling group two,

69
00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:30.198
just to test it out from the ALB side

70
00:02:30.198 --> 00:02:33.770
and see if our new application is working fine.

71
00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:36.500
If it is, then we can slowly shift traffic

72
00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:38.130
between the two target groups.

73
00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:41.980
Then eventually we retire the first target group.

74
00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:44.170
The beautiful thing here is that the clients,

75
00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:46.197
still access the only one ALB.

76
00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:49.170
So there's no caching issue on the K inside

77
00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:52.660
with the ALB is the one thing that is splitting traffic,

78
00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:55.540
shifting traffic from your first Auto Scaling group

79
00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:57.320
to your second Auto Scaling group.

80
00:02:57.320 --> 00:02:59.960
So there's a different solution architecture.

81
00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:02.930
Number three, there is the more complex one.

82
00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:04.740
So we have our ALB number one,

83
00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:06.820
that is linked to our Auto Scaling group

84
00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:09.430
with our EC2 instances and launch template v1

85
00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:11.115
and we'll do is that we'll create

86
00:03:11.115 --> 00:03:14.580
an entirely new auto Application Load Balancer

87
00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:19.230
and an entirely new ASG with the launch template number two.

88
00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:20.120
The idea is now we have

89
00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:23.770
two very independent type of architectures.

90
00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:25.320
So what we need to do is to make sure

91
00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:27.780
the clients can talk to our second ALB

92
00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:30.650
and for this we can use route 53.

93
00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:34.020
So we'll define a CNAME and we can use weighted records.

94
00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:36.010
So when the client's makes a DNS query

95
00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:40.060
is going to get both ALBs based on the weight.

96
00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:42.010
So the clients will be doing some load balancing

97
00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:44.510
so part of our clients will be sending traffic

98
00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:46.860
to ALB number one and parts of our clients

99
00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:49.120
will be sending traffic to ALB number two.

100
00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:51.530
That would allow us to have a small amount of traffic

101
00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:53.330
going on to AlB be number two.

102
00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:55.530
The problem here is that we are reeling on

103
00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:57.350
the clients being well behaved

104
00:03:57.350 --> 00:04:00.012
and making DNS queries and respecting the TTL.

105
00:04:00.012 --> 00:04:03.970
So we have to do a slow migration slowly, slowly, slowly.

106
00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:06.850
So there is some risk here, that bad behaving clients

107
00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:08.630
will still be talking to our ALB number one

108
00:04:08.630 --> 00:04:11.990
even though we have told them to switch to ALB number two

109
00:04:11.990 --> 00:04:13.860
with route 53.

110
00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:16.210
The advantage though of this architecture

111
00:04:16.210 --> 00:04:20.010
is that we are able to independently test the ALB number two

112
00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:23.340
before we put it in service at the route 53 level.

113
00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:25.690
So it's possible for us to set up test clients

114
00:04:25.690 --> 00:04:27.040
to do some manual testing,

115
00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:28.980
to do some load testing on their architecture

116
00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:30.580
making sure everything is great

117
00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:32.990
before we have our client do some load balancing

118
00:04:32.990 --> 00:04:37.600
and switch from ALB1 slowly transition to ALB2.

119
00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:39.606
So what I wanna show you as part of this lecture

120
00:04:39.606 --> 00:04:42.927
is that there's not only one way of doing things in AWS.

121
00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:46.790
The kind of architectures you are going to choose

122
00:04:46.790 --> 00:04:49.582
depends on how you feel about complexity

123
00:04:49.582 --> 00:04:52.710
or the requirements of your solution architecture.

124
00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:54.600
How you want to update your applications

125
00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:57.210
and how many components do you want to involve in it.

126
00:04:57.210 --> 00:04:58.905
So through this one example

127
00:04:58.905 --> 00:05:01.320
I wanna show you that in the exam

128
00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:04.035
it is very likely that you have multiple answers

129
00:05:04.035 --> 00:05:05.470
that are going to be good.

130
00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:08.720
Multiple answers will be correct at the exam.

131
00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:11.270
But one of those will be the most appropriate

132
00:05:11.270 --> 00:05:13.010
based on what you're trying to achieve.

133
00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:14.330
This is what you have to identify

134
00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:16.570
and this is what makes this

135
00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:19.650
Solution Architect Professional exam very tricky.

136
00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:20.970
But I hope that was helpful for this lecture,

137
00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:22.490
I hope all of it makes sense

138
00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:24.710
and you're thinking more like a Solutions Architect now

139
00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:26.660
and I will see you in the next lecture.

